2026-09-12, T5f lane. The live run_one_t5f.sh is UNCHANGED; the proposed file is
a separate, clearly-named copy and nothing in the live path was touched.

THE ARGUMENT THAT IS NOT A BUDGET ARGUMENT.  An intermediary either PROPAGATES
the child's exit status or SUBSTITUTES its own.  `mpirun` and `/usr/bin/time`
propagate; `timeout` SUBSTITUTES -- 124 on expiry, and its own death when killed.
That substitution is how STATUS.T5F_CUBE_m came to assert `rc=137` about a solver
that never failed.  Removing the `timeout` does not merely remove a cap: with no
substituting intermediary left, `RC=$?` after the solver IS the solver's status.
THE DELETION RESTORES THE rc CAPTURE.

FOUR PARTS, DRIVEN
  (1) the `timeout` wrapper                       DELETED from the solve path
  (2) `[ "$WALL" -ge "$TIMEOUT_S" ] && CAPPED=1`  DELETED (live file line 366)
      and the CAP_ENFORCED.txt write              DELETED, not disabled
  (3) `cap_enforcement=none` recorded EXPLICITLY; the cap number still printed
      as `cap_core_min_ESTIMATE_ONLY`; wall_s and core_min as DATA
  (4) the solver is a DIRECT child of the waiting shell; `solver_rc` and
      `rc_source` say where the number came from; a wrapper status is never
      named `rc`; and if this shell dies NO STATUS IS WRITTEN AT ALL

THE THREE ARMS, MEASURED
  ARM A   a COMPLETED f at 56,400 s wall against TIMEOUT_S 47,952 -- 8,448 s PAST
          the cap -- writes cap_enforcement=none, note=clean, core_min=940.000 as
          DATA, and NO CAP_ENFORCED.txt.  Under the live file it would have
          written CAP_ENFORCED.txt claiming it was STOPPED at the cap.
  ARM B   the arming guard, extracted by `sed` from the proposed file and driven
          five ways: clean case ARMED_OK; `0/` REFUSE; time dir REFUSE;
          log.solve REFUSE; no 0.orig REFUSE.  Both directions.
  ARM C1  DISEASE: parent waits on `timeout`, guard kill -9'd -> parent records
          rc=137 for a child that exited 0.  CURE: parent waits on the child
          directly, zero intermediaries -> rc=0.  The exact shape of tonight.
  ARM C2  parent killed mid-run -> NO STATUS.  The heartbeat wrote PROGRESS.txt
          and contained NO rc, because it never learns one.

HOW TO VERIFY THIS LANDED -- AND THE CHECK THAT LOOKS RIGHT AND IS WRONG.
  WRONG:   grep -c 'timeout' run_one_t5f.sh            -> expects 0
  Why it is wrong: the file LEGITIMATELY keeps ~12 mentions of `timeout` -- in
  comments, and in the `--drive-cap-kill` selftest arms (:115-131) which are a
  DRIVEN PROOF that a cap can kill.  Deleting that arm would remove a control,
  not a guard, and a check that demands 0 would push the next reader to do it.
  CORRECT: grep -cE 'timeout.*SOLVER_PATH' run_one_t5f.sh -> MUST be 0
  That tests the only thing that matters: nothing stands between the waiting
  shell and the solver that could SUBSTITUTE its own exit status.

WHERE THIS IS WRONG, NAMED BY ITS AUTHOR

1. NOTHING STOPS A RUNAWAY ANY MORE, AND THAT IS THE POINT AND THE RISK.  A
   diverging f now runs until it finishes, the box dies, or a human stops it.
   The registered cap becomes an estimate nobody enforces.  That is the
   directive, but it is a real loss of a real guard and it is not a free change.

2. THE HEARTBEAT IS A PLAIN SUBSHELL AND DIES WITH THIS SHELL.  That is
   deliberate -- it must not outlive the only process entitled to write an rc --
   but it means PROGRESS.txt goes stale silently, and a reader cannot tell a
   finished run from a killed one without checking the process.  It records
   liveness, never completion.

3. `/usr/bin/time` IS STILL AN INTERMEDIARY.  It propagates on the normal path,
   which is why it is kept (peak RSS is measured nowhere else), but if IT is
   killed the shell records ITS status.  The mitigation is part 4's absent-not-
   wrong property, not immunity.  A future rung wanting immunity must drop it.

4. IT DOES NOT AND CANNOT REPAIR STATUS.T5F_CUBE_m.  That file is written, it
   carries rc=137, and `analyse_t5e.py:534-536` fails the level on it as
   PHYSICS-CRITICAL.  This diff prevents the NEXT one; it fixes nothing already
   on disk, and no part of it should be read as making m gradeable.

5. ARM A IS DRIVEN ON LINES EXTRACTED BY `sed` FROM THE PROPOSED FILE, NOT ON
   THE FILE RUN END-TO-END.  Running it end-to-end needs a real solver and a real
   case, which is a launch.  The extraction is mechanical and the lines are the
   file's own, but it is not the same as an integration test and is not claimed
   to be.  ARM B has the same caveat; only C1/C2 are self-contained.

7. THREE KEYS ARE RENAMED AND A GRADER WILL NOTE ONE ABSENT.  `capped` ->
   `cap_enforcement`, `cap_core_min` -> `cap_core_min_ESTIMATE_ONLY`,
   `timeout_s` -> `timeout_s_NOT_ENFORCED`.  No reader CONSUMES any of them
   (`_l342_class` classifies names for printing and never opens a STATUS file),
   but the loop at analyse_t5e.py:537-540 notes the ABSENCE of named keys, so it
   will now print "capped absent from STATUS: NOT MEASURED (infrastructure)".
   THAT LINE IS EXPECTED AND IS NOT A DEFECT.  The renames are deliberate: a
   field called `capped` on a run nothing can cap is a lie waiting to be quoted.

6. ARM B COULD NOT BE DRIVEN THROUGH THE REAL FILE.  `--dry-run --level c` hit
   the SEQUENTIAL guard first, because T5F_CUBE_m is live, and returned before
   the arming guard was reached.  So the sequential guard is proven on the real
   file; the arming guard is proven only on its extracted lines.

